WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.940
Good morning, Hank, it's Friday.

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:02.920
Couple things. Thing one: in re:your last video,

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:07.080
it is not an anchoring bias to dismiss the opinions of people who don't like The Mountain Goats.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.400
It's just a correct aesthetic judgement.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.320
Hank, there are just facts in this world, like the fact that my Sing-A-Ma-Jig compositions

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.260
[plays music]

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.580
are excellent, but inferior to Mozart.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:19.620
And here's another fact: The Mountain Goats are a great band!

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.720
And *yes* my voice cracked; I'm angry!

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.220
Thing two: Hank, your video on Wednesday reminded me of my favorite kind of bias, the denomination effect.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.940
The denomination effect shows that people are biased towards spending money in small denominations,

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:35.060
like, we're more likely to spend one hundred one dollar bills than we are to spend one one hundred dollar bill.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.880
It's also shown that across the world people are biased towards spending coins

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.260
rather than spending paper money.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.960
Although not American pennies of course, because no one spends American pennies

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.360
because they're worthless and evil.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.700
You wanna an anchoring bias, Hank, I have an anchoring bias against people who don't hate pennies.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.760
So Hank, you know why I hate pennies: it's because they cost more than a penny to make

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.960
and they also don't work as money because they don't facilitate the exchange of goods and services.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.360
Like, try going to a gas station and spending three hundred eighty-three pennies on a gallon of gas.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.360
Lots of countries have eliminated their pennies.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.300
Like, the Netherlands has no pennies
and you know what happened? Nothing.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:08.600
Inflation didn't go up, charitable contributions
didn't go down. Pennies are useless!

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.500
Also if we got rid of the penny it would save the government millions of dollars a year.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.880
It's the kind of thing that you would do
if you were trying to, say, cut the budget

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.940
and you were serious about it
instead of just playing political games.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.820
Right, I'm getting distracted by my hatred of pennies.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.780
The denomination effect does not apply to pennies
but it applies to most coins!

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.000
Y'know, coins that have value.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.540
Which brings me to the one dollar coin.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:28.260
Hank, there are like a billion one dollar coins
sitting around the United States

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.400
but no one ever uses them
because people like paper money.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.440
But the non-partisan government accountability office recently reported to Congress

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.540
that if we eliminated the one dollar bill
and introduced one dollar and two dollar coins

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.340
we would save 5.5 billion dollars over the next thirty years.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.480
Billion with a B.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.060
This is for two reasons: First, it costs a lot of money to print one dollar bills.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.960
It now costs 9.6 cents per bill
thanks to the rising price of cotton.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.180
And secondly, the average one dollar bill is only
in circulation for forty months,

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.660
whereas the average one dollar coin would be
in circulation for like, thirty years.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:01.440
And we wouldn't even have to mint any new coins because we already have a billion one dollar coins

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.900
that no one is using. We just need to remove the bills
so that people have to use them.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.520
And if we did that people might be slightly inconvenienced for a while,

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.700
though they seemed to have gotten used to it
in Canada and Europe.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.920
But aside from saving the federal government
five billion dollars,

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:17.040
the denomination effect tells us that people
would also spend more money!

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.820
And consumer spending is the number one driver
of economic growth in America.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.980
In short, Hank, there are a lot of hard choices to make, but the denomination effect reminds us

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.320
that there are also some easy choices to make,
and we aren't making them.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.360
Okay Hank, couple other things:

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.420
First, Nerdfighters, thank you so much for
continuing to make The Fault in Our Stars

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.100
a bestseller on Amazon and Barnes and Noble.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.800
I don't... even know what to say about that, but...
you're made of awesome.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.120
Also I want to thank Nerdfighters
Julie and Martha for making

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:44.700
WillAllPreordersOfTheFaultInOurStarsBeSigned.com,
my favorite website ever!

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.240
You'll find a link in the doobly-doo.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.420
It succinctly answers the question
I've been asked hundreds of times this week.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:52.060
Speaking of which, please don't forget
that *you* are deciding

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.060
which color sharpie I will use to sign these books!

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.800
Nerdfighters, this is serious business. There is a link in the doobly-doo to a poll in your pants

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:03.240
-- is not something people say about serious business.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.480
No really, I do want to get the color right, so vote.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.820
Oh, and don't forget to read the first six chapters of
The Great Gatsby by Wednesday!

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.420
I know you don't come on YouTube for homework
but you just got some!

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:12.760
Hank, I'll see you on Monday.

